 \reviewcomment{3.1} The ``semantic correctness'' metric means only ``executes without runtime errors,'' not result equivalence; a seed that runs but computes something different still counts. Using ``+130-450\%'' as a headline overstates what was measured.
 
 \reply
-We have renamed ``Semantic Correctness'' to ``Execution Validity'' throughout the paper, including the abstract, the introduction, and Table~V. The headline improvements (e.g., 130\% over \sedar{} and 100\%--450\% over the baselines) are therefore reported as gains in execution validity rather than semantic correctness. We have also clarified in Section~IV (Evaluation) that execution validity measures executability on the target DBMS and does not imply result equivalence, and we have added a sampled result and state equivalence study, in which \tool{} achieves 52.0\% result equivalence and 74.0\% state equivalence, compared with 38.0\% and 69.0\% for \sedar{}. Details can be found in \metareviewref{1}.
